Right-to-left and mixed text, as a browser shows it (Unicode Bidirectional Algorithm, UAX #9). A platform shaper gives
a line's glyphs in visual order: the right-to-left runs reversed, the glyph x positions out of text order, and, when
the first strong letter is right-to-left, the whole line laid out as a right-to-left paragraph. Wrapping, painting and
hit tests assume that x grows with the byte index, so a wrapped line lost its width: a fragment drew at the window's
left edge and the rest of the paragraph was missing. Now `layout_wrapped_line` shapes a line that holds right-to-left
text, or whose paragraph is right to left, in its paragraph direction (a direction mark before it when its first
strong letter has the other one), puts the glyphs into text order with each cluster's advance
(`bidi::logical_layout`), wraps that, and lays out each row in visual order (`bidi::BidiRows`, with `unicode-bidi`). A
wrapped line paints its glyphs, underlines, strikethroughs and backgrounds at those places, and finds an index from a
point and a point from an index there. Text without right-to-left letters in a left-to-right paragraph keeps its old
layout. The paragraph direction is the new `TextStyle::direction` (`TextDirection`, `Styled::text_direction`,
`WindowTextSystem::shape_text_in`), left-to-right unless set, as HTML without `dir`. A text element's hit tests also
take its `text_align` into account (`WrappedLineLayout::row_offset`, which the bidi rows are also painted at), as its
painting does, and `TextLayout::closest_index_for_position` gives the nearest character boundary as a selection takes
it. `first_strong_direction` gives a text's direction as HTML's `dir="auto"` takes it, and
`WrappedLineLayout::bidi_glyph_origins` (test-support) where a line in the bidi path paints each glyph
(`BidiRows::glyphs`, which painting reads). A point on the bottom edge of a line is on the next line. Line breaks
between words come from upstream (UAX #14, `icu_segmenter`). `tests/bidirectional_text.rs` tests it with a stand-in
shaper (the crate's own `--lib` tests do not build from the crates.io package). Our own.

